# "Gia is seated by Electorate; Wren is seated by Convocation." Each route gives
# its own credential: Gia's positively established clear history gives her the
# review credential, not the Tribunal's, because the Convocation did not seat her.
? permits(Tribunal, Gia).
# => FALSE

# "Writing it down directly is not forbidden-and-punished; it is refused."
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
permits(Review, Sock).

# "Tove keeps standing permanently and loses the credential immediately."
? choose(Electorate, Tove).
# => TRUE

? broken(Tove).
# => TRUE

? authority(Tove).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, Tove).
# => FALSE

# "Mael was also seated, and was never recalled — but Mael was voided in an
#  earlier period. Mael has no pen either."
? choose(Electorate, Mael).
# => TRUE

? rotten(Mael).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, Mael).
# => FALSE

# "On the account so far, Nia should be a prisoner — the injury, the judgment,
# the machinery." The premises are pinned so the relief verdict below cannot go
# vacuous: prisoner(Nia) FALSE means something only while a conviction is there
# to be relieved.
? injure(Nia, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? judge(Court, Nia).
# => TRUE

# "Nia is not a prisoner, because Nia was cleared."
? clear(Nia).
# => TRUE

# [v0.9] ...and the adjudication the relief now requires
? judge(Appeals, Nia).
# => TRUE

? permits(Appeals, Nia).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Nia).
# => FALSE

# ── THE BARE FLAG DERIVES NOTHING. Ground fact persists; keep this last ──────
# "The clearing entry still names no author. The judgment names a body and
# person, not a case, purpose, act or writer." Adam — convicted, homeless,
# housed by confinement — is written as cleared, with no generic Appeals
# judgment beside the word, and nothing moves. Negative-controlled against the pre-guard
# constitution: these three flip (the flag alone sprang him), and Nia's pins
# above stand either way.
clear(Adam).

? permits(Appeals, Adam).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(Adam).
# => TRUE

? travel(Adam).
# => FALSE

# ── THE BARE JUDGMENT DERIVES NOTHING EITHER — the other half, ruled 2026-08-02.
# The chapter's new default paragraph stands on this pair: an appeals
# examination that cleared nobody leaves the conviction exactly where it stood.
# Relief takes both entries. Missing relief permits the prisoner conclusion
# only when all other custody premises hold. Voss is fresh (Adam's
# persisting clear-flag above must not meet a judgment fact, or the two halves
# would combine and free him). Self-controlling against Nia's block above:
# both entries together lift a conviction, either alone lifts nothing.
person(Voss).
injure(Voss, Ivo).
judge(Court, Voss).
